</code></pre> <p>Use BigQuery for batch prediction when your input data is already in a BigQuery table. The batch prediction job and your table must be in the same region.</p> Step 1: Prepare BigQuery inputs <p></p> <p>Input requirements</p> <ul> <li>Permissions: Your service account must have the BigQuery User role. See Prerequisites.</li> <li>Required column: A <code>request</code> column is required and must be of the JSON type. This JSON data represents your input for the model and must match the structure of a <code>GenerateContentRequest</code>.</li> <li>Other columns: Your input table can have other columns with any BigQuery data type except array, struct, range, datetime, and geography. These columns are ignored for content generation but are included in the output table.</li> <li>Important: Do not use <code>response</code> or <code>status</code> as column names. These are reserved keywords.</li> </ul> <p>Example input (JSON in <code>request</code> column)</p> <p>The following JSON object is an example of what a single cell in the <code>request</code> column would contain.</p> <pre><code>{
